/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/aws-developer.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("aws-developer", [["dva1",0,1,"A Lambda function consumes an SQS queue in batches of 10. When one message fails, all 10 are returned to the queue and reprocessed. How can the developer retry only the failed messages?",["Raise the queue's visibility timeout to 12 hours so retries happen less often","Convert the standard queue to a FIFO queue with content-based deduplication","Set the function's reserved concurrency to 1 so batches run one at a time","Enable ReportBatchItemFailures and return the failed message IDs"],3,"With ReportBatchItemFailures the function returns batchItemFailures, so Lambda deletes the successes and only the listed messages become visible again. A longer visibility timeout just delays the same full-batch retry.","Task 1.2",["A longer visibility timeout only delays the retry; the whole batch of 10 still becomes visible again when one message fails.","FIFO with content-based deduplication prevents duplicate sends, but a failed batch is still retried as a whole without partial batch responses.","Reserved concurrency of 1 only serializes batches; each failing batch is still returned to the queue in full.",null],null],["dva2",0,1,"An orders table must return all orders for one customer, newest first, using a single efficient request. Which key design fits?",["Partition key orderDate and sort key customerId","Partition key customerId and sort key orderDate","Partition key orderId with no sort key","Partition key status and sort key customerId"],1,"A Query on partition key customerId returns that customer's items, and ScanIndexForward=false sorts them by orderDate descending. Using orderId alone would force a Scan.","Task 1.3",["With orderDate as the partition key, one customer's orders are scattered across many partition values and cannot be fetched with a single Query.",null,"With only orderId as the key, finding one customer's orders requires a Scan, and there is no sort key to order by date.","A status partition key groups orders by status, not by customer, and a low-cardinality key like status also creates hot partitions."],null],["dva3",0,1,"A DynamoDB table already exists and holds data. The team now needs to query items by an email attribute that is not part of the primary key. What should they add?",["A local secondary index with email as its sort key","A DynamoDB Streams trigger on the table","A global secondary index with email as its partition key","A second attribute named email in the table's primary key"],2,"GSIs can be added to an existing table and can use any attribute as a new partition key. LSIs can only be defined when the table is created and must share the table's partition key.","Task 1.3",["An LSI can only be created together with the table and must reuse the table's partition key, so it cannot be added now or be keyed on email.","Streams capture item changes for processing; they do not give you a way to query items by email.",null,"A table's primary key cannot be changed after creation; you would have to create a new table and migrate the data."],null],["dva4",0,1,"Which DynamoDB read operation cannot use strongly consistent reads?",["A Query against a global secondary index","A GetItem on the base table","A Query against a local secondary index","A Scan of the base table"],0,"GSIs are updated asynchronously and support only eventually consistent reads. Base-table reads and LSI queries can request ConsistentRead=true.","Task 1.3",[null,"GetItem on the base table supports ConsistentRead=true, so strongly consistent reads are available there.","LSIs share the base table's partition and support strongly consistent reads when ConsistentRead=true is set.","A Scan of the base table can also request ConsistentRead=true, so it does support strong consistency."],null],["dva5",0,1,"An application performs 10 strongly consistent reads per second of items that are 6 KB each. How many read capacity units does the table need?",["20","10","15","60"],0,"One RCU covers one strongly consistent read per second of up to 4 KB. 6 KB rounds up to 8 KB, which is 2 RCUs per read, so 10 reads need 20. Eventually consistent reads would need 10.","Task 1.3",[null,"10 RCU would cover 6 KB items only with eventually consistent reads; strongly consistent reads of 6 KB need 2 RCU each.","15 comes from dividing 6 KB by 4 KB without rounding up; capacity is billed in whole 4 KB units, so each read costs 2 RCU.","60 treats RCU as 1 KB units (the write unit size); read units are 4 KB, so 6 KB needs 2 RCU per read."],null],["dva6",0,1,"An application writes 12 items per second to DynamoDB, and each item is 2.5 KB. How many write capacity units are required?",["30","36","12","24"],1,"One WCU covers one write per second of up to 1 KB. 2.5 KB rounds up to 3 KB, so 3 WCUs per write, times 12 writes, gives 36.","Task 1.3",["30 uses 2.5 KB without rounding up; each write is billed in whole 1 KB units, so 2.5 KB costs 3 WCU.",null,"12 would only be enough for items of 1 KB or less; a 2.5 KB item needs 3 WCU per write.","24 rounds 2.5 KB down to 2 KB; DynamoDB always rounds item size up, to 3 KB here."],null],["dva7",0,1,"Two users edit the same DynamoDB item at the same time, and one user's change silently overwrites the other's. How should the developer prevent lost updates?",["Switch every read in the application to eventually consistent mode","Add a global secondary index on a version attribute and query it first","Turn on DynamoDB Streams and replay the lost changes from the stream","Store a version number and add a condition expression that checks it on write"],3,"Optimistic locking compares the expected version in a ConditionExpression; if another writer changed it first, the write fails with ConditionalCheckFailedException instead of overwriting.","Task 1.3",["Read consistency affects how fresh a read is, not whether a later write overwrites a concurrent one; eventual reads make the problem worse.","A GSI on a version attribute is eventually consistent and a separate read, so a race between read and write remains.","Streams record changes after they happen; replaying them does not stop the overwrite and cannot decide which edit should win.",null],null],["dva8",0,1,"A Lambda function opens a new database connection and loads a large config file on every invocation, adding latency. What change helps most?",["Increase the function timeout to 15 minutes so the setup has time to finish","Copy the config file into the function's /tmp folder at the start of every call","Create the connection and load the config outside the handler for reuse","Invoke the function asynchronously so callers do not wait for the setup work"],2,"Code outside the handler runs once per execution environment and is reused by warm invocations. A longer timeout or async invocation does not remove the repeated setup work.","Task 1.2",["A longer timeout lets the slow setup finish but still repeats it on every invocation, so latency stays the same.","Copying the file to /tmp on every call is still repeated work; /tmp persists in warm environments, but only code outside the handler avoids redoing it.",null,"Async invocation hides latency from callers but the function still repeats the same setup on every run and costs the same duration."],null],["dva9",0,1,"A nightly batch job takes about 40 minutes to run in a single process. The team wants a serverless option with no servers to manage. Where should it run?",["A single Lambda function with the maximum timeout","An API Gateway REST API with a Lambda integration","A Lambda@Edge function on CloudFront","An Amazon ECS task on AWS Fargate"],3,"Lambda functions stop after at most 15 minutes, so a 40-minute single process does not fit. Fargate runs containers for as long as needed without managing EC2 instances.","Task 1.2",["Lambda's maximum timeout is 15 minutes, so a 40-minute single process would be stopped partway through.","API Gateway integrations time out after about 29 seconds by default, and Lambda behind it is still capped at 15 minutes.","Lambda@Edge has even tighter limits (seconds, not minutes) and is meant for request/response processing at CloudFront, not batch jobs.",null],null],["dva10",0,1,"An S3 event invokes a Lambda function asynchronously. Events that still fail after Lambda's built-in retries are being lost. What should the developer configure?",["Configure an on-failure destination or a dead-letter queue","Increase the function's memory so the retries are more likely to succeed","Configure provisioned concurrency so every event gets a warm environment","Lower the maximum event age to 60 seconds so failures are reported sooner"],0,"For asynchronous invocations, Lambda retries twice by default and then discards the event unless an on-failure destination or DLQ is set. A shorter event age would drop events even sooner.","Task 1.2",[null,"More memory may help some retries succeed, but events that still fail after the retries are still discarded without a destination or DLQ.","Provisioned concurrency reduces cold starts; it does nothing to capture events that fail processing.","Lowering the maximum event age makes Lambda discard events sooner; without a destination or DLQ they are still lost."],null],["dva11",0,1,"A Lambda function attached to private subnets in a VPC must call a public third-party API on the internet. What is required?",["A public IP address assigned to the function's elastic network interfaces","An internet gateway route added directly to the private subnets' route table","A NAT gateway in a public subnet, routed from the private subnets","A VPC gateway endpoint that points to the third-party API's domain name"],2,"Lambda ENIs in a VPC never get public IPs, so outbound internet access goes through a NAT gateway. Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB. Note that NAT gateways are billed hourly.","Task 1.2",["Lambda's elastic network interfaces in a VPC never receive public IP addresses, and you cannot assign one.","A route to an internet gateway makes the subnets public, but the function's ENIs still have no public IP, so traffic cannot get out.",null,"Gateway endpoints exist only for S3 and DynamoDB; they cannot reach arbitrary third-party internet APIs."],null],["dva12",0,1,"Twelve Lambda functions use the same in-house logging library. How can the team share it without bundling a copy into each deployment package?",["Store the library's code in an environment variable read by each function","Publish the library as a Lambda layer and attach it to each function","Download the library into each function's /tmp folder on every cold start","Run the library as a separate state in an AWS Step Functions state machine"],1,"Layers package shared libraries or runtimes once and attach them to many functions. Environment variables hold small strings, not code.","Task 1.2",["Environment variables are limited to 4 KB in total and are meant for configuration strings, not library code.",null,"Downloading on each cold start adds latency and an external dependency, and it still keeps a copy per function; layers solve this directly.","Step Functions orchestrates separate tasks; a logging library must run inside each function's process, not as a workflow state."],null],["dva13",0,1,"A developer wants a workflow that charges a card, waits (possibly for days) for a human to approve a refund, then continues, with retries on each step. Which service fits best?",["A single Lambda function that polls in a loop","An SQS standard queue with a long visibility timeout","Amazon EventBridge with a scheduled rule","AWS Step Functions with a task token callback"],3,"Step Functions Standard workflows can pause on a .waitForTaskToken task until SendTaskSuccess is called, and they provide per-state retry and catch. A polling Lambda would hit the 15-minute limit.","Task 1.1",["A single Lambda function stops after 15 minutes, so it cannot wait days, and polling in a loop wastes compute.","SQS visibility timeout tops out at 12 hours and a queue has no workflow logic, per-step retries or approval callbacks.","A scheduled EventBridge rule runs on a timer; it does not track workflow state or pause until a human responds.",null],null],["dva14",0,1,"When an order is placed, three independent services (billing, shipping, analytics) must each receive and process every order event. Which design is most appropriate?",["Send each order to one SQS queue that all three services poll","Write each order to an S3 bucket and have every service list the bucket","Publish to an SNS topic with one SQS queue subscribed per service","Invoke the three services one after another from the producer"],2,"SNS-to-SQS fan-out delivers a copy of each message to every subscribed queue, so each service processes all events at its own pace. With one shared queue, each message would go to only one consumer.","Task 1.1",["With one shared queue each message is delivered to only one of the competing consumers, so each service would miss most orders.","Listing a bucket is slow, has no delivery tracking and forces each service to work out which objects it has already processed.",null,"Invoking services in sequence couples the producer to all three and a failure or slowdown in one blocks or breaks the rest."],null],["dva15",0,1,"An SQS FIFO queue carries updates for many customers. Updates must stay in order for each customer, but different customers should be processed in parallel. What should the producer set?",["The MessageDeduplicationId to the customer ID","The MessageGroupId to the customer ID","The DelaySeconds to a different value for each customer","A separate FIFO queue for every message"],1,"FIFO ordering is kept within a message group, and different groups can be consumed in parallel. The deduplication ID only prevents duplicates within the 5-minute window.","Task 1.1",["The deduplication ID suppresses repeated messages with the same ID within 5 minutes, so using the customer ID would drop that customer's later updates.",null,"DelaySeconds only postpones delivery; FIFO queues support only a queue-level delay, and it has no effect on per-customer ordering or parallelism.","A queue per message is unmanageable and loses ordering across a customer's messages; message groups do this within one queue."],null],["dva16",0,1,"A consumer polls an SQS queue in a tight loop and receives many empty responses, increasing cost. What should the developer change?",["Enable long polling with a receive wait time of up to 20 seconds","Lower the queue's message retention period to reduce the number of messages","Shorten the visibility timeout to 0 seconds so messages come back faster","Convert the queue to a FIFO queue so messages are delivered in batches"],0,"Long polling waits for messages to arrive before responding, cutting empty receives and request charges. Visibility timeout and retention do not affect empty polls.","Task 1.1",[null,"Retention controls how long unconsumed messages are kept; it does not reduce empty responses from polling an empty queue.","A visibility timeout of 0 makes in-flight messages reappear immediately and causes duplicates; it does not reduce empty receives.","FIFO queues change ordering and deduplication; they still return empty responses under short polling."],null],["dva17",0,1,"Messages in an SQS queue are sometimes processed twice. Processing a message takes about 90 seconds, and the queue's visibility timeout is 30 seconds. What is the most likely fix?",["Enable long polling on the queue so consumers wait for new messages","Increase the message retention period to the maximum of 14 days","Raise the visibility timeout above the typical processing time","Add a 60-second delivery delay to each message the producer sends"],2,"If processing outlasts the visibility timeout, the message becomes visible again and another consumer picks it up. Setting the timeout longer than processing time (or extending it with ChangeMessageVisibility) prevents that.","Task 1.1",["Long polling reduces empty receives; it does not stop a message from reappearing while it is still being processed.","Retention decides how long unprocessed messages are kept, which is unrelated to duplicates caused by a short visibility timeout.",null,"A delivery delay only affects when a new message first becomes visible; after it is received, the 30-second visibility timeout still expires mid-processing."],null],["dva18",0,1,"A cleanup Lambda function must run every night at 02:00 UTC. What is the simplest way to trigger it?",["An Amazon EventBridge schedule with a cron expression","An SQS delay queue with a 24-hour delay","A CloudWatch alarm on the function's Invocations metric","An S3 lifecycle rule that invokes Lambda"],0,"EventBridge Scheduler (or a scheduled rule) invokes targets on cron or rate expressions. SQS delays are capped at 15 minutes.","Task 1.1",[null,"SQS delay queues cap at 15 minutes, so a 24-hour delay is not possible, and it would not run on a fixed clock time.","A CloudWatch alarm reacts to metric thresholds; it is not a scheduler and cannot invoke Lambda directly at a set time.","S3 lifecycle rules transition or expire objects; they cannot invoke Lambda or run on a schedule."],null],["dva19",0,1,"An application's AWS SDK calls occasionally fail with ThrottlingException during traffic spikes. What should the code do?",["Retry immediately in a tight loop until it succeeds","Retry with exponential backoff and jitter","Catch the error and discard the request","Create a new SDK client for each retry"],1,"AWS recommends exponential backoff with jitter so retries spread out and give the service time to recover. Immediate retries add load and make throttling worse.","Task 1.1",["Immediate retries in a tight loop add even more load while the service is already throttling, so failures continue.",null,"Discarding requests loses data or work; throttling is a transient error that should be retried with backoff.","A new client does not change the service's rate limit and wastes time on setup; the fix is spacing out retries."],null],["dva20",0,1,"A clickstream must be processed in order per user, kept for several days so it can be replayed, and read by multiple independent applications. Which service fits?",["Amazon SQS standard queue","Amazon SNS standard topic","Amazon SES","Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"],3,"Kinesis Data Streams keeps records ordered per shard (by partition key), retains them for replay and lets several consumers read the same data. SQS messages are removed once consumed.","Task 1.1",["SQS standard queues do not guarantee order, and a consumed message is deleted, so it cannot be replayed or shared by several apps.","SNS pushes messages to subscribers but does not store them for replay, and standard topics do not guarantee order.","Amazon SES is an email-sending service and has nothing to do with streaming clickstream data.",null],null],["dva21",0,1,"Which caching strategy updates the cache every time the database is written, so reads are never stale, at the cost of caching data that may never be read?",["Write-through","Lazy loading","Time-to-live only","Read replica"],0,"Write-through writes to the cache along with the database. Lazy loading fills the cache only on a miss, so it caches only requested data but can serve stale values.","Task 1.3",[null,"Lazy loading fills the cache only on a read miss, so it caches only requested data and can serve stale values after writes.","A TTL only expires entries after a set time; it does not update the cache when the database is written.","A read replica is a database copy for scaling reads; it is not a caching strategy and can lag behind the primary."],null],["dva22",0,1,"A DynamoDB table stores web sessions that should disappear 24 hours after creation without any cleanup code. What should the developer use?",["A nightly Scan that deletes old items with BatchWriteItem calls","A global secondary index on the session creation date attribute","DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) with a 24-hour item cache lifetime","DynamoDB TTL on an attribute that holds the expiry timestamp"],3,"TTL deletes items after the epoch time in the chosen attribute passes, at no extra write cost. A Scan-and-delete job works but consumes capacity and needs code.","Task 1.3",["A nightly Scan-and-delete job works but needs code, consumes read and write capacity, and removes items up to a day late.","A GSI only lets you query by creation date; it does not delete anything, so cleanup code would still be needed.","DAX caches reads in front of the table; a cache lifetime does not delete items from DynamoDB itself.",null],null],["dva23",0,1,"Whenever an item in a DynamoDB table changes, a Lambda function must send a notification that includes the old and new values. What should the developer configure?",["A CloudTrail trail that records DynamoDB data events for the table","DynamoDB Streams (new and old images) with a Lambda trigger","A scheduled Lambda function that scans the whole table every minute","An SNS topic subscribed directly to the table's change notifications"],1,"Streams record each item change, and the NEW_AND_OLD_IMAGES view type includes both versions; Lambda polls the stream through an event source mapping.","Task 1.3",["CloudTrail data events record who called which API, not the item's old and new attribute values, and they are not a push trigger for Lambda.",null,"A per-minute full Scan is expensive, misses intermediate changes and never sees the item's previous values.","DynamoDB cannot publish change notifications directly to an SNS topic; item-level changes are exposed through Streams."],null],["dva24",0,1,"An application uploads 3 GB video files to S3 and occasionally has to restart the whole upload after a network blip. What should it use?",["A single PutObject call with a longer timeout","S3 Transfer Acceleration without changing the upload method","Multipart upload, retrying only the failed parts","S3 Object Lock in governance mode"],2,"Multipart upload sends parts in parallel and lets a failed part be retried alone; AWS recommends it above about 100 MB and it is required above 5 GB. Transfer Acceleration can speed the path but still restarts a single PUT.","Task 1.1",["A single PutObject is limited to 5 GB and a network blip still forces the whole 3 GB upload to restart, whatever the timeout.","Transfer Acceleration can speed up the network path, but a single PUT that fails still has to start over from zero.",null,"Object Lock prevents objects from being deleted or overwritten; it does nothing to make uploads resumable."],null],["dva25",0,1,"A report runs a Scan with a FilterExpression on customerId, which is the table's partition key, and it is slow and expensive. What is the better approach?",["Increase the page size of the Scan","Use a parallel Scan with more segments","Use a Query with a key condition on customerId","Add a FilterExpression on another attribute as well"],2,"A Scan reads every item and filters afterward, consuming capacity for the whole table. A Query on the partition key reads only the matching items.","Task 1.3",["A larger page size only changes how many items come back per call; the Scan still reads and bills the whole table.","A parallel Scan finishes sooner but still reads every item and uses even more capacity at once.",null,"Filters are applied after the items are read, so another FilterExpression does not reduce the capacity consumed."],null],["dva26",0,1,"A read-heavy DynamoDB application needs microsecond read latency for repeated reads of the same items, without changing its data-access code much. What should the developer add?",["DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)","A DynamoDB global table","An S3 bucket in front of the table","DynamoDB Streams"],0,"DAX is an API-compatible in-memory cache for DynamoDB that serves repeat reads in microseconds. Global tables replicate across Regions for availability, not caching.","Task 1.3",[null,"Global tables replicate data across Regions for availability and local writes; they do not add an in-memory cache.","S3 is object storage with millisecond latency and would require rewriting the data-access code; it is not a DynamoDB cache.","Streams deliver a log of item changes for processing; they do not speed up reads."],null],["dva27",0,1,"An API Gateway REST API with Lambda proxy integration returns 502 Bad Gateway. The function returns the plain string \"OK\". What is wrong?",["Enable CORS on the method so browsers accept the plain response","Return an object with statusCode, headers and a string body","Raise the function timeout above the 29-second integration limit","Add a mapping template that wraps the string in a JSON object"],1,"With proxy integration, API Gateway expects a JSON object containing statusCode and body (body as a string). Any other shape is a malformed Lambda response, which becomes a 502.","Task 1.1",["CORS only controls which browser origins may read a response; it does not fix the malformed proxy response that API Gateway turns into a 502.",null,"A timeout would produce a 504, not a 502, and the function already returns quickly; the problem is the shape of what it returns.","Proxy integrations pass the request and response through without mapping templates, so API Gateway still requires statusCode and body from the function."],null],["dva28",0,1,"An SQS standard queue can deliver the same message more than once. How should the consumer that charges customers be designed?",["Make it idempotent by storing each request ID and skipping repeats","Delete each message as soon as it is received, before charging the card","Set the visibility timeout to 0 seconds so duplicates show up at once","Switch the consumer's database reads to strongly consistent mode"],0,"Standard queues guarantee at-least-once delivery, so consumers must tolerate duplicates, for example with a conditional write on a request ID. Deleting first risks losing the message if processing fails.","Task 1.1",[null,"Deleting before processing means a crash mid-charge loses the message entirely, and it still does not stop a duplicate delivery from being charged.","A zero visibility timeout makes messages reappear immediately while still being processed, which causes more duplicate charges, not fewer.","Read consistency affects how fresh database reads are; it does not stop the same SQS message from being delivered and charged twice."],null],["dva29",0,2,"A Lambda function needs to read items from one DynamoDB table. How should it get permissions?",["Store an IAM user's access keys in the function's environment variables","Add a table policy that lets any principal in the account read the table","An execution role whose policy allows read actions on that one table","Attach the AdministratorAccess managed policy to the function's role"],2,"Lambda assumes its execution role and gets temporary credentials automatically; scoping the policy to the table follows least privilege. Long-lived IAM user keys are a risk and unnecessary.","Task 2.1",["Long-lived IAM user keys can leak and must be rotated by hand; Lambda already gets temporary credentials from its execution role.","Opening the table to every principal in the account breaks least privilege, and the function still needs an identity to make the calls.",null,"AdministratorAccess would work but grants far more than reading one table, violating least privilege."],null],["dva30",0,2,"An application on an EC2 instance must upload files to an S3 bucket. What is the recommended way to give it credentials?",["Save an IAM user's access keys in ~/.aws/credentials on the instance","Attach an IAM role to the instance through an instance profile","Hardcode the access keys in the application's source code","Make the bucket public so no credentials are needed"],1,"An instance profile delivers temporary, automatically rotated role credentials through the instance metadata service, and the SDK finds them without code changes.","Task 2.1",["Access keys stored on the instance are long-lived secrets that can leak and must be rotated manually; an instance role avoids them.",null,"Hardcoded keys end up in source control and builds, and are the most common way credentials leak.","Making the bucket public exposes its data to anyone, and uploads would need public write access, which violates least privilege."],null],["dva31",0,2,"A mobile app's users sign in with a Cognito user pool. The app then needs temporary AWS credentials to upload photos directly to S3. What provides those credentials?",["The user pool access token, sent to S3 in place of a SigV4 signature","A separate IAM user with its own access keys for every mobile user","A Cognito identity pool that trades the user pool token for role credentials","An API Gateway API key that the app includes in its S3 upload requests"],2,"User pools authenticate users and issue JWTs; identity pools federate those identities into temporary AWS credentials for an IAM role. S3 does not accept user pool tokens directly.","Task 2.1",["S3 only accepts SigV4-signed requests with AWS credentials; a user pool access token is a JWT that S3 does not understand.","An IAM user per mobile user does not scale (IAM has user quotas) and puts long-lived keys on devices.",null,"API keys identify clients for API Gateway usage plans; S3 does not use them and they are not AWS credentials."],null],["dva32",0,2,"A REST API in API Gateway must accept only requests from users signed in to an existing Cognito user pool, with the least custom code. What should the developer configure?",["A Cognito user pool authorizer on the API methods","A Lambda authorizer that validates the JWT","IAM authorization with SigV4 signing","An API key required on every method"],0,"A Cognito user pool authorizer validates the user pool token without custom code. A Lambda authorizer would also work but requires writing and maintaining validation logic.","Task 2.1",[null,"A Lambda authorizer could validate the JWT, but it requires writing and maintaining custom code, which the question wants to avoid.","IAM authorization needs AWS credentials and SigV4 signing; user pool sign-in alone does not give the client IAM credentials.","API keys only identify clients for usage plans; anyone with the key can call the API, so they do not prove a user signed in."],null],["dva33",0,2,"An API must validate bearer tokens issued by a third-party identity provider that uses a custom, non-standard token format. Which API Gateway feature fits?",["A Lambda authorizer","A Cognito user pool authorizer","A resource policy that limits source IPs","A usage plan with throttling"],0,"A Lambda authorizer runs your code to validate any token format and returns an IAM policy. Cognito authorizers only accept Cognito user pool tokens.","Task 2.1",[null,"A Cognito user pool authorizer accepts only tokens issued by a Cognito user pool, not a third party's custom format.","A resource policy can restrict source IPs, accounts or VPC endpoints, but it cannot validate a bearer token.","Usage plans throttle and meter clients identified by API keys; they do not validate tokens."],null],["dva34",0,2,"A backend service running with an IAM role must call an API Gateway API, and only that role should be allowed. Which authorization type should the API use?",["A Cognito user pool authorizer that checks the caller's ID token","An API key sent in the x-api-key header of every request","IAM authorization, with callers signing requests using SigV4","No authorization on the API, with an AWS WAF rule on the stage"],2,"IAM authorization checks the SigV4 signature against IAM policies, so access can be limited to a specific role. API keys identify clients for usage plans and are not an authorization mechanism.","Task 2.1",["Cognito authorizers validate user pool tokens for end users; a backend service with an IAM role has no user pool token.","API keys identify clients for usage plans; they can be shared or leaked and do not prove the caller is a specific IAM role.",null,"Leaving the API unauthenticated lets anyone call it; WAF filters traffic patterns but does not authenticate an IAM role."],null],["dva35",0,2,"An application in account A must write to a DynamoDB table in account B. What is the recommended approach?",["An IAM user in account B whose access keys are copied into account A","Account A's root user added to an administrators group in account B","A table resource policy in account B that allows any principal to write","A role in account B that trusts account A, assumed with sts:AssumeRole"],3,"Cross-account access uses a role whose trust policy names account A; the caller also needs sts:AssumeRole permission and receives temporary credentials. Sharing long-lived keys is not recommended.","Task 2.1",["Copying long-lived IAM user keys between accounts is a security risk and needs manual rotation; roles give temporary credentials.","The root user should never be used for applications, and root users cannot be added to IAM groups in another account.","A policy allowing any principal to write would open the table to everyone, not just account A, which breaks least privilege.",null],null],["dva36",0,2,"An application uses an Amazon RDS database password that must be rotated automatically every 30 days with minimal custom work. Where should the password be stored?",["Systems Manager Parameter Store as a String parameter","AWS Secrets Manager with rotation enabled","An encrypted environment variable on the Lambda function","A text file in a private S3 bucket"],1,"Secrets Manager has built-in rotation for RDS credentials. Parameter Store can hold SecureStrings but has no built-in rotation, so you would build it yourself.","Task 2.3",["A plain String parameter is not encrypted, and Parameter Store has no built-in rotation for RDS passwords.",null,"Lambda environment variables have no rotation feature; you would have to update and redeploy the value yourself.","A file in S3 has no rotation, and the application would have to fetch and parse it; it is not a secrets store."],null],["dva37",0,2,"A team needs to store feature settings and a few encrypted API endpoints that never rotate, at the lowest cost. What should they use?",["AWS Secrets Manager, with a separate secret created for every single value","Parameter Store standard parameters, with SecureString for sensitive values","Amazon DynamoDB items encrypted on the client with the Encryption SDK","A separate AWS KMS customer managed key that stores each setting value"],1,"Standard Parameter Store parameters have no extra storage charge and SecureString encrypts with KMS. Secrets Manager bills per secret per month and mainly adds rotation, which is not needed here.","Task 2.3",["Secrets Manager charges per secret per month and mainly adds rotation, which these static values do not need.",null,"Encrypting items in DynamoDB with the Encryption SDK works but adds code and table costs that Parameter Store avoids.","KMS keys encrypt data; they do not store configuration values, and each customer managed key costs about $1 per month."],null],["dva38",0,2,"An application must encrypt 50 MB files with a KMS key. Calling the KMS Encrypt API fails because of the payload size. What should the developer do?",["Split the file into 4 KB chunks and call the KMS Encrypt API for each chunk","Ask AWS Support to raise the Encrypt payload limit for the account","Use GenerateDataKey and encrypt the file locally with the data key","Store the file unencrypted and use KMS to encrypt only the S3 object key"],2,"KMS Encrypt accepts at most 4 KB. Envelope encryption uses a data key to encrypt the data locally and keeps only the wrapped key, which KMS can decrypt later.","Task 2.2",["Chunking into 4 KB calls would work but is slow, expensive and throttle-prone; envelope encryption is the intended pattern.","The 4 KB Encrypt limit is a fixed service limit, not an adjustable quota that Support can raise.",null,"Encrypting only the object key name leaves the file contents in plaintext, which does not meet the requirement."],null],["dva39",0,2,"A compliance team wants every use of the key that encrypts S3 objects to appear in CloudTrail, and wants to control the key policy themselves. Which encryption option fits?",["SSE-KMS with a customer managed key","SSE-S3 with S3 managed keys","SSE-C with keys supplied on every request","No server-side encryption, relying on TLS"],0,"SSE-KMS logs KMS API calls in CloudTrail, and a customer managed key lets the team write the key policy. SSE-S3 keys are managed entirely by S3 and have no key policy you control.","Task 2.2",[null,"SSE-S3 keys are fully managed by S3; there is no key policy to control and no per-use KMS entries in CloudTrail.","With SSE-C, the customer supplies the key on each request and S3 does not store it; there is no KMS key policy or KMS key-usage log.","TLS protects data only in transit; objects would sit unencrypted at rest and there would be no key usage to audit."],null],["dva40",0,2,"An application writes thousands of objects per second to a bucket that uses SSE-KMS and starts receiving KMS throttling errors. What reduces KMS requests with the least change?",["Switch to client-side encryption with the Encryption SDK","Create a new KMS key for each object","Disable default encryption on the bucket","Enable S3 Bucket Keys for the bucket"],3,"An S3 Bucket Key creates a short-lived bucket-level key that S3 uses to derive data keys, cutting calls to KMS and their cost.","Task 2.2",["Client-side encryption with the Encryption SDK still calls KMS for data keys unless caching is added, and it requires significant code changes.","A key per object multiplies the number of keys and KMS calls, making throttling and cost worse.","Disabling default encryption does not remove the SSE-KMS requirement set by the application or policy, and would weaken protection.",null],null],["dva41",0,2,"A bucket policy must reject any request that is not sent over HTTPS. Which condition should a Deny statement use?",["s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption equal to AES256","aws:SourceIp not in the corporate range","aws:SecureTransport equal to false","s3:signatureversion equal to AWS"],2,"Denying requests where aws:SecureTransport is false enforces encryption in transit. The server-side encryption header controls encryption at rest, not transport.","Task 2.2",["The server-side encryption header governs encryption at rest; it says nothing about whether the request used HTTPS.","aws:SourceIp restricts where requests come from, not whether they are encrypted in transit.",null,"s3:signatureversion checks the signing version (SigV2 or SigV4), not whether the connection uses TLS."],null],["dva42",0,2,"A web app lets users upload a profile photo directly to a private S3 bucket without giving them AWS credentials. What should the backend generate?",["A public-read ACL on the bucket for the duration of the upload","An IAM user with access keys created for each end user","A CloudFront signed cookie that grants read access to the bucket","A presigned PutObject URL that expires after a few minutes"],3,"A presigned URL carries the signer's permission for one operation on one object until it expires, so the bucket stays private.","Task 2.2",["A public-read ACL exposes the bucket's objects to everyone and does not grant upload permission, so the bucket would no longer be private.","Creating IAM users and keys for end users hands out long-lived AWS credentials, which is exactly what the app must avoid.","Signed cookies grant read access to content through CloudFront; they do not let a user upload an object to the bucket.",null],null],["dva43",0,2,"A developer is attaching an ACM certificate to a CloudFront distribution for a custom domain, but the certificate does not appear in the list. What is the most likely reason?",["ACM certificates cannot be used with CloudFront","The certificate was requested in a Region other than US East (N. Virginia)","The certificate must be imported, not requested","CloudFront needs the certificate stored in Secrets Manager"],1,"CloudFront only uses ACM certificates from us-east-1. For a Regional API Gateway endpoint or ALB, the certificate must be in the same Region as the resource.","Task 2.2",["ACM certificates are the standard way to add a custom-domain TLS certificate to CloudFront; they just have to be in us-east-1.",null,"Both ACM-issued and imported certificates work with CloudFront; importing is not required.","CloudFront reads certificates from ACM (or IAM), not from Secrets Manager."],null],["dva44",0,2,"A developer's script works locally because it reads access keys hardcoded in the source file. The script will run on Lambda next. What is the best practice?",["Remove the keys and let the SDK use the function's execution role","Move the same access keys into the function's environment variables","Encrypt the hardcoded keys with KMS and decrypt them on each call","Store the keys in a config file packaged in the deployment .zip"],0,"The SDK's default provider chain finds role credentials automatically on Lambda, EC2 and ECS. Environment variable keys are still long-lived secrets that can leak.","Task 2.3",[null,"Environment variables still hold long-lived keys that can leak through the console, logs or the function configuration.","Encrypting the keys still leaves long-lived credentials to manage and rotate, when the execution role provides temporary ones for free.","A config file in the package is still a hardcoded long-lived secret that anyone with access to the package can read."],null],["dva45",0,2,"S3 must invoke a Lambda function when objects are created, but the event notification fails to save with a permissions error. What is missing?",["An s3:PutBucketNotification permission in the function's execution role","A resource-based policy on the function that lets S3 invoke it","A bucket policy that grants the function s3:GetObject on new objects","SSE-KMS encryption enabled as the source bucket's default encryption"],1,"Services that push events to Lambda need permission in the function's resource-based policy (lambda add-permission). The execution role controls what the function itself can do.","Task 2.1",["The execution role controls what the function can do, not who may invoke it; S3 needs permission in the function's resource-based policy.",null,"A bucket policy governs access to objects; the failure is that S3 is not allowed to invoke the function.","Bucket encryption settings have nothing to do with S3's permission to invoke a Lambda function."],null],["dva46",0,2,"A user has an IAM policy allowing s3:* on a bucket, and the bucket policy explicitly denies s3:DeleteObject for that user. What happens when the user tries to delete an object?",["The request is denied, because an explicit deny overrides any allow","The request succeeds, because the identity policy allows s3:*","The request succeeds only if the object is unencrypted","The result depends on which policy was created most recently"],0,"In IAM policy evaluation, an explicit deny anywhere wins over any allow. Order of creation is irrelevant.","Task 2.1",[null,"An allow never overrides an explicit deny; the s3:* allow is cancelled for DeleteObject by the bucket policy's deny.","Encryption status has no effect on this evaluation; the explicit deny applies to every DeleteObject call by that user.","Policy evaluation does not depend on creation order; all applicable policies are evaluated together and explicit deny wins."],null],["dva47",0,2,"After signing in to a Cognito user pool, which token contains claims about the user such as email and name for the app to display?",["The access token","The refresh token","The session token from STS","The ID token"],3,"The ID token carries identity claims about the user. The access token carries scopes and groups for authorizing API calls, and the refresh token gets new tokens.","Task 2.1",["The access token carries scopes and groups for authorizing API calls, not the profile claims meant for display.","The refresh token is opaque and is only used to obtain new ID and access tokens.","An STS session token belongs to temporary AWS credentials (for example from an identity pool) and holds no user profile claims.",null],null],["dva48",0,2,"A Lambda function in account A must decrypt data with a customer managed KMS key in account B. What permissions are needed?",["Only an IAM policy in account A that allows kms:Decrypt on the key","Only a key policy in account B that names account A as a principal","A key policy in B granting account A, and an IAM policy in A","A multi-Region replica of the key created directly in account A"],2,"Cross-account KMS access needs both sides: the key policy grants the other account, and an IAM policy in that account grants the principal. Either alone is not enough.","Task 2.2",["Without the key policy in account B allowing account A, the IAM policy alone cannot grant access to a key in another account.","The key policy lets account A delegate access, but the function's role in account A still needs an IAM policy allowing kms:Decrypt.",null,"Multi-Region replica keys are created in other Regions of the same account, not in a different account."],null],["dva49",0,2,"Data must be encrypted inside the application before it is sent to any AWS service, so AWS never sees plaintext. What should the developer use?",["Client-side encryption with the AWS Encryption SDK","SSE-S3 default encryption configured on each bucket","SSE-KMS server-side encryption with an AWS managed key","TLS 1.2 or later enforced on every service connection"],0,"Client-side encryption encrypts data before it leaves the application. Server-side options encrypt after the service receives the data, and TLS protects data only in transit.","Task 2.2",[null,"SSE-S3 encrypts only after S3 receives the data, so AWS still sees the plaintext in transit to the service.","SSE-KMS is still server-side encryption; S3 receives the plaintext and encrypts it on arrival.","TLS protects data in transit only; the service decrypts it on arrival and sees plaintext."],null],["dva50",0,2,"Application logs in CloudWatch Logs sometimes contain customer email addresses. The team wants those values masked automatically. What should they use?",["A CloudWatch metric filter","A shorter log retention period","A subscription filter to Kinesis","A CloudWatch Logs data protection policy"],3,"Data protection policies detect and mask sensitive data such as email addresses in log events. Metric filters only count patterns, and shorter retention does not hide the data.","Task 2.3",["A metric filter counts or extracts values into metrics; it does not mask the email addresses in the log events.","Shorter retention deletes logs sooner but the emails remain visible until then.","A subscription filter streams log events elsewhere, copying the sensitive data rather than masking it.",null],null],["dva51",0,2,"A developer wants to replace a broad policy on a role with one that grants only the actions the application actually used in the last 90 days. Which tool helps generate it?",["AWS Trusted Advisor cost optimization checks","IAM Access Analyzer policy generation","Amazon Inspector vulnerability findings for the role","AWS Config conformance packs applied to the account"],1,"IAM Access Analyzer can review CloudTrail logs for a role and generate a policy with only the used actions. Inspector scans workloads for vulnerabilities, not permissions.","Task 2.1",["Trusted Advisor cost checks look for idle or underused resources, not unused IAM permissions.",null,"Inspector scans EC2, ECR images and Lambda for vulnerabilities; it does not generate IAM policies.","Config conformance packs evaluate resource configuration against rules; they do not build a policy from actual API usage."],null],["dva52",0,3,"A developer has an AWS SAM template and wants to build the code and deploy it for the first time, answering prompts for the stack name and Region. Which commands should be run?",["sam init, then sam local invoke","cdk synth, then cdk deploy","sam build, then sam deploy --guided","aws s3 cp, then aws lambda invoke"],2,"sam build prepares the artifacts and sam deploy --guided asks for settings and saves them to samconfig.toml for later runs. sam local invoke only runs a function locally.","Task 3.1",["sam init creates a new project from a template and sam local invoke runs a function locally; neither deploys to AWS.","cdk synth and cdk deploy belong to the AWS CDK, not to an existing SAM template.",null,"Copying files to S3 and invoking a function does not build or deploy the SAM stack at all."],null],["dva53",0,3,"A developer wants to run a Lambda function locally with a sample event before deploying it. Which tool supports this?",["aws lambda invoke against the $LATEST version","CloudFormation change sets","sam local invoke with an event file (using Docker)","CodeDeploy in-place deployment"],2,"SAM CLI emulates the Lambda runtime in a Docker container, so the function can be tested locally with a JSON event. aws lambda invoke calls the deployed function in AWS.","Task 3.2",["aws lambda invoke calls the function already deployed in AWS, so it cannot test code before it is deployed.","Change sets preview CloudFormation resource changes; they do not execute function code.",null,"CodeDeploy deploys code to AWS compute targets; it does not run a function locally for testing."],null],["dva54",0,3,"A CloudFormation template references local Lambda code folders. Before deploying with the AWS CLI, what must happen to that code?",["Paste the code inline into the ZipFile property of every function resource","Run aws cloudformation package to upload it to S3 and rewrite the paths","Store the code in Parameter Store and reference each parameter by name","Copy the code to an EC2 instance that CloudFormation reads it from"],1,"The package command zips local artifacts, uploads them to an S3 bucket and outputs a template with S3 locations for deploy to use.","Task 3.1",["Inline ZipFile code works only for small single-file functions (up to 4 KB) and is not how local code folders are packaged.",null,"Parameter Store holds configuration values, not deployment packages, and CloudFormation cannot load function code from it.","CloudFormation cannot read code from an EC2 instance; function code must come from S3, an image in ECR or inline."],null],["dva55",0,3,"A team deploys a new Lambda version and wants 10% of traffic to go to it for 5 minutes, then all traffic if no alarms fire, with automatic rollback. What should they use?",["CodeDeploy with Canary10Percent5Minutes on an alias","An all-at-once update of the function's $LATEST version","Two separate functions behind an ALB with equal target weights","A new Lambda layer that holds the new code for the function"],0,"CodeDeploy shifts alias weights between versions following the canary configuration and rolls back if CloudWatch alarms go off. Updating $LATEST changes all traffic at once.","Task 3.4",[null,"Updating $LATEST sends 100% of traffic to the new code at once with no canary period or automatic rollback.","Weighted ALB targets could split traffic, but there is no automatic 5-minute canary step or alarm-driven rollback.","A layer holds shared libraries; it does not control traffic between versions or roll back."],null],["dva56",0,3,"Which CodeDeploy traffic-shifting option for Lambda moves traffic in equal increments at fixed intervals, such as 10% every minute?",["Canary","All-at-once","Blue/green with CNAME swap","Linear"],3,"Linear shifts equal percentages at regular intervals until 100%. Canary shifts one first percentage, waits, then moves the rest.","Task 3.4",["Canary shifts one initial percentage, waits, then moves all remaining traffic at once, rather than equal steps.","All-at-once moves 100% of traffic immediately with no increments.","A CNAME swap is an Elastic Beanstalk blue/green technique, not a CodeDeploy traffic-shifting configuration for Lambda.",null],null],["dva57",0,3,"An Elastic Beanstalk production app must keep full capacity during deployments, and a failed deployment must roll back quickly by terminating the new instances. Which deployment policy fits?",["All at once","Immutable","Rolling","Rolling with an additional batch"],1,"Immutable deployments launch new instances in a temporary Auto Scaling group, so the old ones keep serving; rollback just terminates the new group. Rolling reduces capacity during the update.","Task 3.4",["All at once takes every instance out of service together, causing downtime and a slow redeploy to roll back.",null,"Rolling updates instances in batches, so capacity drops during deployment and rollback means redeploying.","Rolling with an additional batch keeps capacity, but it updates existing instances, so rollback requires another deployment rather than terminating new ones."],null],["dva58",0,3,"A developer wants the fastest possible Elastic Beanstalk deployment for a development environment and can accept brief downtime. Which policy should be used?",["Immutable","Traffic splitting","All at once","Rolling with an additional batch"],2,"All at once updates every instance at the same time, which is fastest but causes an outage while instances restart.","Task 3.4",["Immutable launches a whole new set of instances, which is safe but the slowest policy.","Traffic splitting runs new instances alongside old ones for an evaluation period, which takes longer.",null,"Rolling with an additional batch launches extra instances and updates in batches, which is slower than all at once."],null],["dva59",0,3,"A team runs version 2 in a separate Elastic Beanstalk environment and wants to cut over with almost no downtime and an easy way back. What should they do?",["Swap the environment URLs (CNAME swap) between the two environments","Deploy version 2 to the existing environment with the all-at-once policy","Delete the old environment and then rename the new one","Change the Auto Scaling group's minimum size to zero"],0,"A blue/green swap exchanges the CNAMEs so traffic moves to the new environment; swapping back restores the old one.","Task 3.4",[null,"An all-at-once deployment to the existing environment causes downtime and has no quick way back.","Deleting the old environment removes the easy rollback path, and environments cannot simply be renamed to take over the URL.","Setting minimum size to zero could shut down capacity; it does not move traffic to version 2."],null],["dva60",0,3,"A pipeline in CodePipeline must pause after deploying to staging until a team lead confirms the release. What should be added?",["A CodeBuild test action that runs automatically after the staging deploy","A CloudWatch alarm that stops the pipeline until it is cleared","A longer timeout on the staging deploy action in the pipeline","A manual approval action between the staging and production stages"],3,"Manual approval actions stop the pipeline until someone approves or rejects, optionally sending an SNS notification.","Task 3.4",["An automated test action runs without a person; it cannot wait for a team lead's confirmation.","CloudWatch alarms do not pause a pipeline for human sign-off; a manual approval action is the built-in way to do that.","A longer action timeout only lets the deployment run longer; it does not pause the pipeline for someone to approve.",null],null],["dva61",0,3,"In a CodeBuild buildspec.yml, where should unit tests that must run after the code compiles be placed?",["In the install phase runtime-versions section","In the build or post_build phase commands","In the artifacts files section","In the env variables section"],1,"Phases run in order: install, pre_build, build, post_build. Tests after compilation belong in build or post_build; artifacts only lists output files.","Task 3.3",["runtime-versions selects language runtimes during install, which happens before the code is compiled.",null,"The artifacts section lists output files to keep; it does not run commands.","The env section defines environment variables and runs no commands."],null],["dva62",0,3,"A CodeDeploy deployment to EC2 must run integration tests after the application has started. Which appspec.yml hook should run the test script?",["ValidateService","BeforeInstall","ApplicationStop","DownloadBundle"],0,"ValidateService is the last hook of an EC2/on-premises deployment and runs after ApplicationStart, making it the place to verify the service. DownloadBundle is reserved for the agent and cannot run scripts.","Task 3.3",[null,"BeforeInstall runs before the new files are installed, so the application is not running yet.","ApplicationStop runs first, to stop the old version, well before the new application starts.","DownloadBundle is run by the CodeDeploy agent itself and cannot run user scripts."],null],["dva63",0,3,"A CodeDeploy deployment to a new set of EC2 instances fails immediately with no lifecycle events recorded. What is the most likely cause?",["The revision bundle is stored in S3 instead of a GitHub repository","The CodeDeploy agent is not installed or not running on the instances","The deployment group selects the instances by EC2 tag instead of ASG","The appspec.yml file does not define a ValidateService lifecycle hook"],1,"On EC2 the CodeDeploy agent carries out deployments; without it, no lifecycle events run. Bundles in S3 and tag-based groups are normal.","Task 3.4",["Storing the revision in S3 is a normal, supported source for CodeDeploy and does not stop lifecycle events from running.",null,"Tag-based deployment groups are a normal way to target EC2 instances and do not prevent lifecycle events.","ValidateService is optional; leaving it out would not stop all lifecycle events from running."],null],["dva64",0,3,"An API Gateway API has dev and prod stages, and each stage must call a different alias of the same Lambda function. What should the developer use?",["A separate AWS account for each stage","Two different Lambda functions with identical code","Stage variables referenced in the Lambda integration ARN","Usage plans attached to each stage"],2,"The integration can reference ${stageVariables.alias}, so each stage calls its own alias. Remember to allow API Gateway to invoke each alias.","Task 3.4",["Separate accounts per stage would still leave each stage needing its own integration target; stage variables do this within one API.","Duplicating functions creates code drift and ignores aliases, which exist to point to versions of one function.",null,"Usage plans throttle and meter API keys per stage; they cannot change which Lambda alias the integration calls."],null],["dva65",0,3,"A developer changed several REST API methods in the API Gateway console, but clients still see the old behavior. What step was missed?",["Enabling CORS on the API","Creating a new usage plan","Deploying the API to the stage","Turning on stage caching"],2,"REST API changes take effect only after a deployment to a stage creates a new snapshot.","Task 3.4",["CORS controls which browser origins may call the API; it does not publish changed methods.","Usage plans manage throttling and quotas for API keys; they do not activate method changes.",null,"Stage caching could serve old responses, but it is off by default; the missing step is deploying the changes to the stage."],null],["dva66",0,3,"A Lambda function's dependencies add up to 1.5 GB, which is too large for a .zip deployment package. How can it still run on Lambda?",["Package it as a container image in Amazon ECR","Split the dependencies across six Lambda layers","Upload the .zip to S3 instead of directly","Store the libraries in environment variables"],0,"Container images up to 10 GB are supported. .zip packages, including layers, are limited to 250 MB unzipped, so extra layers do not help.","Task 3.1",[null,"Layers count toward the same 250 MB unzipped limit as the function package, so splitting across layers does not help.","Uploading via S3 raises the upload size limit, but the 250 MB unzipped limit still applies.","Environment variables are limited to 4 KB in total and cannot hold libraries."],null],["dva67",0,3,"Before updating a production CloudFormation stack, a developer wants to see exactly which resources will be modified or replaced. What should they use?",["Stack drift detection","A stack policy","Termination protection","A change set"],3,"A change set lists the proposed changes, including replacements, before you execute it. Drift detection compares current resources to the last deployed template instead.","Task 3.4",["Drift detection compares live resources with the last deployed template; it does not preview a new update.","A stack policy protects resources from being updated or replaced, but it does not show what a change will do.","Termination protection only prevents the stack from being deleted.",null],null],["dva68",0,3,"A networking stack creates a VPC, and application stacks must reference its subnet IDs without hardcoding them. What should the developer use?",["Fn::GetAtt with the networking stack's name to read its subnets","Subnet IDs copied into each application template's Mappings by hand","Outputs exported by the networking stack, read with Fn::ImportValue","A DependsOn attribute that points at the networking stack"],2,"Cross-stack references export an output from one stack and import it by name in another. Fn::GetAtt only works within one template.","Task 3.1",["Fn::GetAtt only reads attributes of resources in the same template; it cannot reach into another stack.","Copying IDs into Mappings is hardcoding, and the templates break whenever the networking stack changes.",null,"DependsOn only orders resource creation within one template; it cannot reference another stack or pass values."],null],["dva69",0,3,"A team defines infrastructure in TypeScript with the AWS CDK. Which command produces the CloudFormation template without deploying it?",["cdk bootstrap","cdk deploy","cdk destroy","cdk synth"],3,"cdk synth synthesizes the app into CloudFormation templates. cdk bootstrap creates resources the CDK needs in an account and Region.","Task 3.1",["cdk bootstrap provisions the staging resources (such as an S3 bucket and roles) the CDK needs in an account and Region.","cdk deploy synthesizes and then deploys the stacks, which the question wants to avoid.","cdk destroy deletes deployed stacks.",null],null],["dva70",0,3,"A front-end team needs an API endpoint that returns a fixed sample JSON response so they can build the UI before the backend exists. What is the quickest option?",["A Lambda function with provisioned concurrency","An Application Load Balancer with fixed targets","An API Gateway mock integration","A DynamoDB table with a sample item"],2,"Mock integrations return a response defined in API Gateway without calling any backend, which suits early testing.","Task 3.2",["A Lambda function still has to be written and deployed, and provisioned concurrency only cuts cold starts at extra cost.","An ALB fixed-response rule can return static text, but it adds an hourly-billed load balancer and is slower to set up than a mock integration in the API itself.",null,"A DynamoDB table is only storage; you would still need an API and code to return the item."],null],["dva71",0,3,"A team wants to roll out a configuration change to their application gradually, validate it against a schema, and roll back automatically if an alarm fires, without redeploying code. What should they use?",["A new Lambda version for each setting change","AWS AppConfig with a deployment strategy and validators","Environment variables updated with the CLI","A CloudFormation stack policy"],1,"AppConfig deploys configuration and feature flags with gradual strategies, validators and CloudWatch alarm rollback. Changing environment variables redeploys the function configuration all at once.","Task 3.4",["Publishing a new version for each setting change is a code deployment, with no schema validation or gradual rollout of the setting.",null,"Updating environment variables changes the configuration for all invocations at once, with no validator or alarm rollback.","A stack policy prevents updates to protected resources; it does not roll out configuration gradually."],null],["dva72",0,3,"Several teams want to host private npm and PyPI packages and proxy the public registries for their builds. Which service fits?",["AWS CodeArtifact","Amazon ECR","AWS CodeDeploy","Amazon S3 Glacier"],0,"CodeArtifact is a managed artifact repository for npm, PyPI, Maven and more, with upstream connections to public registries. ECR stores container images.","Task 3.1",[null,"ECR stores container images, not npm or PyPI packages, and does not proxy those public registries.","CodeDeploy deploys applications to compute targets; it is not a package repository.","S3 Glacier is archival storage with slow retrieval and no package-manager protocol support."],null],["dva73",0,4,"A developer uses X-Ray and wants to filter traces by customer tier (for example, tier = gold). Where should that value be recorded?",["As metadata on the segment","In the function's environment variables","As an annotation on the segment or subsegment","In the trace ID header"],2,"Annotations are indexed key-value pairs you can use in filter expressions. Metadata is stored with the trace but is not indexed for search.","Task 4.2",["Metadata is stored with the trace but is not indexed, so filter expressions cannot search on it.","Environment variables configure the function and are not recorded on traces, so X-Ray cannot filter by them.",null,"The trace ID header only propagates trace context between services; you cannot add custom values to filter by."],null],["dva74",0,4,"An application on EC2 is instrumented with the X-Ray SDK, but no traces appear in X-Ray. What is the most likely cause?",["The X-Ray daemon or agent is not running, or the role cannot write traces","Active tracing is not turned on for the instance in the Lambda console","The instance has no Elastic IP address, so traces cannot leave it","CloudTrail is disabled in the Region, so trace data is not collected"],0,"On EC2 the SDK sends segments over UDP to a local daemon or agent, which uploads them using the instance role. Lambda's active tracing setting does not apply to EC2.","Task 4.1",[null,"Active tracing is a Lambda setting; it does not apply to an EC2 instance.","Traces reach X-Ray through the daemon or agent using normal outbound connectivity; no Elastic IP is required.","CloudTrail records API activity and is unrelated to collecting X-Ray trace segments."],null],["dva75",0,4,"A Lambda function must publish custom business metrics to CloudWatch without making synchronous PutMetricData calls on every request. What should it use?",["A CloudWatch Logs subscription to Kinesis","The CloudWatch embedded metric format in its log output","An X-Ray sampling rule","CloudTrail data events"],1,"The embedded metric format writes structured JSON log lines that CloudWatch turns into metrics asynchronously, avoiding extra API calls and latency.","Task 4.2",["A subscription filter streams log events to Kinesis; it does not create CloudWatch metrics from them.",null,"Sampling rules decide which requests X-Ray traces; they do not publish business metrics.","CloudTrail data events log API calls for auditing and do not produce custom application metrics."],null],["dva76",0,4,"During an incident, a developer needs to find the 20 slowest requests from the last hour across many log streams. Which tool fits best?",["AWS Config","Amazon Inspector","IAM Access Analyzer","CloudWatch Logs Insights"],3,"Logs Insights runs queries (filter, stats, sort) across log groups, which suits finding the slowest requests. The other tools are for configuration, vulnerabilities and permissions.","Task 4.1",["AWS Config records resource configuration changes, not application request logs.","Inspector scans workloads for software vulnerabilities and cannot query log events.","Access Analyzer finds unintended resource access and generates policies; it does not search logs.",null],null],["dva77",0,4,"During a traffic spike, synchronous calls to a Lambda function fail with TooManyRequestsException (HTTP 429). What is the most likely cause?",["The function's memory is set too high","The deployment package is larger than 50 MB","The function has hit its concurrency limit","The function's execution role is missing a policy"],2,"429 throttling means no concurrency is available, either the account limit or the function's reserved concurrency. Request a quota increase or adjust reserved concurrency; a missing policy would cause AccessDenied instead.","Task 4.1",["High memory raises cost per millisecond but does not cause 429 throttling errors.","An oversized package fails at deployment time, not with runtime throttling during a traffic spike.",null,"A missing execution-role policy causes AccessDenied errors from the called service, not 429 from Lambda."],null],["dva78",0,4,"A CPU-heavy Lambda function takes 9 seconds at 512 MB of memory. What change is most likely to make it faster?",["Increase the memory setting, which also allocates more CPU","Increase the timeout so the function has more time to finish","Add reserved concurrency so each invocation gets a dedicated core","Move the function's code into a layer so it loads more quickly"],0,"Lambda allocates CPU in proportion to memory, so more memory often shortens CPU-bound runs and can even lower cost. A longer timeout does not add speed.","Task 4.3",[null,"A longer timeout only allows the function to run longer; it does not make the CPU-bound work any faster.","Reserved concurrency limits and guarantees the number of concurrent environments; it does not add CPU to each one.","Layers only package shared code; the code runs the same way and gets no extra CPU."],null],["dva79",0,4,"A latency-sensitive API backed by Lambda sees slow responses after quiet periods because new execution environments must start. What removes most of this delay?",["Provisioned concurrency on the function's alias","A larger /tmp storage setting","Reserved concurrency set to 0","A dead-letter queue"],0,"Provisioned concurrency keeps initialized environments ready, avoiding cold starts. Reserved concurrency of 0 would stop the function from running at all. Provisioned concurrency is billed while it is configured.","Task 4.3",[null,"Ephemeral /tmp storage size does not affect how long a new execution environment takes to initialize.","Reserved concurrency of 0 throttles every invocation, so the function would not run at all.","A DLQ captures failed asynchronous events; it does nothing about cold-start latency."],null],["dva80",0,4,"A DynamoDB table using the date as its partition key throttles writes even though total consumed capacity is well below the table's limit. What is the likely cause and fix?",["The table needs a local secondary index on the date attribute","A hot partition; use a partition key with many distinct values","Strongly consistent reads are enabled on the table's queries","The TTL attribute is missing, so old items are never removed"],1,"All of today's writes land on one partition key value, concentrating traffic. A high-cardinality key (or adding a random suffix) spreads writes across partitions.","Task 4.3",["An LSI must be defined at table creation, shares the same partition key, and adds write load rather than spreading it.",null,"Strongly consistent reads affect read capacity, not write throttling on one partition key value.","TTL deletes expired items but does not spread today's writes across partitions."],null],["dva81",0,4,"A REST API returns the same product catalog to thousands of clients, and each request invokes Lambda and reads DynamoDB. How can the developer reduce backend calls with minimal code?",["Increase the Lambda function's memory","Switch DynamoDB to on-demand capacity","Add a dead-letter queue to the function","Enable API Gateway stage caching with a suitable TTL"],3,"Stage caching returns cached responses for the TTL without invoking the backend. Note that the cache is billed by the hour and is not part of the Free Tier.","Task 4.3",["More memory makes each invocation faster but every request still invokes Lambda and reads DynamoDB.","On-demand capacity changes how DynamoDB is billed and scaled but does not reduce the number of backend calls.","A DLQ captures failed async events and does not reduce requests for the same catalog.",null],null],["dva82",0,4,"Users around the world download static images from an S3 bucket and see high latency. What is the best improvement?",["Enable S3 versioning","Serve the bucket through a CloudFront distribution","Move the images to DynamoDB","Enable S3 Object Lock"],1,"CloudFront caches content at edge locations close to users. Versioning and Object Lock protect data but do not reduce latency.","Task 4.3",["Versioning keeps previous object versions for recovery; it does nothing for download latency.",null,"DynamoDB has a 400 KB item limit and is not suited to serving images, nor would it be closer to users.","Object Lock enforces WORM retention; it does not cache content near users."],null],["dva83",0,4,"A malformed message in an SQS queue keeps failing and being retried, blocking attention from other work. How should the developer isolate such messages?",["Configure a dead-letter queue with a maxReceiveCount","Shorten the queue's message retention period to 1 minute","Turn off long polling so consumers poll more frequently","Increase the batch size to 10 so bad messages are diluted"],0,"A redrive policy moves a message to the DLQ after it has been received maxReceiveCount times, so it can be inspected without endless retries.","Task 4.1",[null,"A 1-minute retention period would delete all slow or unprocessed messages, not just the malformed one.","Turning off long polling only increases empty receives and cost; the bad message still keeps failing.","A larger batch does not isolate the bad message and can cause more good messages to be retried alongside it."],null],["dva84",0,4,"A Lambda function reading a Kinesis data stream shows a steadily rising IteratorAge metric. What does this indicate, and what helps?",["Records are expiring early; reduce the retention period","The function is over-provisioned; lower its memory","The function is falling behind; raise the parallelization factor or add shards","The stream is empty; add a producer"],2,"A growing iterator age means records wait longer before processing. Processing more batches in parallel per shard, adding shards or speeding up the code reduces it.","Task 4.1",["Records expire only after the retention period, and a shorter period would make lagging records expire and be lost.","Lowering memory would make the function slower and increase the lag further.",null,"An empty stream would produce no iterator age growth; a rising age means records are waiting unprocessed."],null],["dva85",0,4,"A developer wants an email when a function's error count exceeds 5 in 5 minutes. What should they configure?",["A CloudWatch alarm on the Errors metric that notifies an SNS email topic","A CloudTrail trail that sends an email for every Lambda API call","An X-Ray group with a filter expression that matches failed traces","A Lambda destination that fires for every successful invocation"],0,"CloudWatch alarms watch a metric against a threshold and can send notifications through SNS.","Task 4.2",[null,"CloudTrail logs Lambda API calls such as function updates, not invocation errors, and emailing every call is not a threshold alert.","An X-Ray group filters traces, but by itself it does not send an email when errors pass a threshold over 5 minutes.","A destination for successful invocations fires on successes, not on an error count threshold."],null],["dva86",0,4,"Requests pass through API Gateway, two Lambda functions and SQS. When a user reports an error, engineers cannot connect the log lines from each component. What logging practice helps most?",["Longer log retention settings on every component's log group","Structured JSON logs carrying a correlation ID for each request","Writing every component's logs to its local /tmp folder instead","Logging only errors and dropping all informational log messages"],1,"A shared correlation (or trace) ID in structured logs lets you search every component for one request, for example with Logs Insights.","Task 4.2",["Longer retention keeps logs around longer but does not link log lines from different components to one request.",null,"/tmp is ephemeral storage local to one execution environment, so logs there are lost and not searchable.","Dropping informational logs removes context and still gives no way to tie events for one request together."],null],["dva87",0,4,"An API Gateway REST API calls a Lambda function that sometimes needs 2 minutes to generate a report, and clients get 504 errors. What is the best design change?",["Raise the Lambda timeout to 15 minutes","Increase the Lambda memory to the maximum","Return a job ID right away and process the report asynchronously","Enable caching on the API stage"],2,"REST API integrations time out at 29 seconds by default, so a 504 occurs no matter how long Lambda may run. An asynchronous job pattern avoids holding the request open.","Task 4.3",["Lambda can run for 15 minutes, but the API Gateway integration still times out at 29 seconds by default and returns 504.","More memory may speed the report up, but a 2-minute job is unlikely to fit within the 29-second integration timeout.",null,"Caching returns stored responses for repeat requests; the first request for each report still times out."],null],["dva88",0,4,"A Lambda function fails with AccessDeniedException when calling s3:GetObject on a bucket in the same account. The bucket has no policy. Where should the developer look first?",["The function's resource-based policy","The function's reserved concurrency","The bucket's CORS configuration","The function's execution role policy"],3,"Calls the function makes are authorized by its execution role. The resource-based policy controls who can invoke the function, and CORS only affects browsers.","Task 4.1",["The resource-based policy controls who can invoke the function, not what the function can access.","Reserved concurrency limits how many copies run; it does not cause AccessDeniedException.","CORS only governs browser cross-origin requests; it does not authorize SDK calls from Lambda.",null],null],["dva89",0,4,"A read-heavy app sends the same expensive SQL queries to an RDS database many times per second, and the results change rarely. What should the developer add?",["A larger RDS instance storage volume","An Amazon ElastiCache cache using lazy loading with a TTL","A CloudWatch Logs subscription filter","A second NAT gateway"],1,"Caching query results in ElastiCache cuts repeated database reads, and a TTL limits how stale results can get.","Task 4.3",["More storage adds capacity for data but does not reduce the repeated query load on the database.",null,"A subscription filter streams logs elsewhere and has no effect on database query load.","A NAT gateway provides outbound internet access for private subnets and does not cache or reduce database queries."],null]]);
